WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.433
The production RAG platform.

2
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.042
It is the most commonly built AI system in industry.

3
00:00:06.042 --> 00:00:09.033
Drawn properly, it is two pipelines and a loop.

4
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:11.633
Here is the whole picture.

5
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:14.500
Ingestion runs offline and never stops.

6
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.867
The query pipeline runs on every request, under latency pressure.

7
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:21.125
And the eval loop watches both.

8
00:00:21.125 --> 00:00:25.167
Most failures come from treating ingestion as a one-time script.

9
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:27.833
Let's start with ingestion.

10
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.336
The parser sets the quality ceiling.

11
00:00:30.336 --> 00:00:33.700
Bad parsing cannot be rescued by a smarter model later.

12
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.387
The chunker is parent-child by default.

13
00:00:36.387 --> 00:00:40.733
We retrieve small precise chunks, but prompt with their larger parents.

14
00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:46.567
Then a dual index: vectors for meaning, BM25 for exact names and codes.

15
00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:49.900
Now the query path, top to bottom.

16
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.367
We retrieve wide, about fifty candidates, merging both indexes.

17
00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.442
The reranker cuts that to five.

18
00:00:56.442 --> 00:00:59.133
It is the biggest quality win per line of code.

19
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:05.500
Prompt assembly adds citation markers and puts the stable prefix first, so provider caching works.

20
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.800
Then the model answers from those chunks.

21
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.600
The loop closes with evals.

22
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:13.833
Any change to chunking or the model is proposed.

23
00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:15.767
It is scored on the golden set.

24
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:18.400
And it ships only if it beats the current score.

25
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.300
Interviewers probe the tradeoffs.

26
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.918
RAG suits a large, changing corpus.

27
00:01:23.918 --> 00:01:26.500
If everything fits in context, skip it.

28
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.619
It suits pointed questions with sources.

29
00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:33.567
For global summaries, consider long context or GraphRAG.

30
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:37.700
And always enforce tenant access at retrieval, never in the prompt.

31
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:41.033
Here is the classic freshness question.

32
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:45.133
A document changes, and full reindexing is too expensive.

33
00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:49.467
A change-detection queue triggers upserts keyed by document id.

34
00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:53.067
Only that one document is re-chunked and re-embedded.

35
00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:55.077
Let's recap.

36
00:01:55.077 --> 00:01:56.900
Two pipelines and one loop.

37
00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.033
Parsing is the quality ceiling.

38
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:02.033
Retrieve wide with hybrid search, then rerank.

39
00:02:02.333 --> 00:02:05.555
And nothing ships without beating the golden set.

40
00:02:05.555 --> 00:02:07.133
Next up: Agent Platform.
